    "whyItMatters": "Cases involving minors or impaired adults become difficult when several people have different forms of authority. The client may express a preference, a parent may pay, a guardian may hold limited powers, a court order may allocate decision-making, and state law may allow a minor to consent to a particular service. The counselor should not decide solely by age, diagnosis, family role, or who scheduled the appointment. The correct process is to identify the decision, assess the client's functional ability, verify legal authority, seek assent when appropriate, protect confidentiality, and revisit the arrangement when circumstances change.",
